proofs.proof_tex:
The upper and lower bounds use the same class \(\mathcal M_n\) and the same public-information decision class \(\mathcal T_n\).  By thm:clipped-absolute-upper and def:absolute-risk,
\[
 \mathfrak R_n^{(1)}
 \leq\sup_{\mathcal M_n}E_P|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)|
 \leq C\rho_n.                                                \tag{57}
\]
For \(n\geq5\), each block size \(m_j\) in def:median-estimator satisfies \(n/9\leq m_j\leq n\).  Repeating the bound of thm:clipped-absolute-upper on that block with the common full-sample threshold changes every sampling factor by at most a class-constant factor: \(m_j^{-1/2}\leq3n^{-1/2}\), \(m_j^{-1/(\kappa+1)}\asymp n^{-1/(\kappa+1)}\), and \((m_jr_{1n}^2)^{-1}\asymp(nr_{1n}^2)^{-1}\).  The propensity-error scale is unchanged.  Hence each block has mean absolute error at most \(K\rho_n\), uniformly over \(\mathcal M_n\).  Lem:median-risk-upgrade then gives
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}
 \{E_P(\widehat\tau_{\mathrm{med}}-\tau(P))^2\}^{1/2}
 \leq\sqrt{21}\,K\rho_n.                                   \tag{58}
\]
For \(1\leq n<5\), \(\lambda_n^\star\geq\min\{\lambda_0,1/4\}\).  Ass:right-overlap bounds the control denominator away from zero.  Ass:outcome-second-moment and Jensen's inequality give \(E_P[g_0(X)^2]\leq M_Y/\eta_+\), and ass:bounded-treatment-effect-signal then gives \(E_P[g_1(X)^2]\leq2M+2M_Y/\eta_+\).  The three radius certificates and \(r_{jn}\leq\bar r\) therefore give a uniform second-moment bound for the full-sample clipped score.  Since \(\rho_n\geq n^{-1/2}\geq1/2\) in this finite set of sample sizes, (58) continues to hold after increasing \(K\).  Def:root-mse-risk now yields \(\mathfrak R_n^{(2)}\leq C\rho_n\).

For fixed \(\delta\in(0,1/4)\), the probability conclusion of thm:clipped-absolute-upper and def:probability-risk give
\[
 \mathfrak R_{n,\delta}^{(\mathrm{prob})}\leq C_\delta\rho_n.\tag{59}
\]

Conversely, thm:four-channel-converse gives, for every \(T\in\mathcal T_n\),
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|T-\tau(P)|\geq c\rho_n\}\geq\delta_0,
\]
as well as the stated absolute-risk and root-mean-squared-risk lower bounds.  If \(0<\delta\leq\delta_0\) and \(u<c\rho_n\), then
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|T-\tau(P)|>u\}
 \geq\sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|T-\tau(P)|\geq c\rho_n\}
 \geq\delta_0\geq\delta.                                    \tag{60}
\]
Taking the two infima in def:probability-risk gives \(\mathfrak R_{n,\delta}^{(\mathrm{prob})}\geq c\rho_n\).  Combining (57)--(60) with the two loss lower bounds in thm:four-channel-converse proves all three uniform equivalences.

For \(n\geq5\), the block calculation in the proof of thm:sharp-four-risk-frontier verifies the premise of lem:median-risk-upgrade with a uniform constant \(K\).  Consequently
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|\widehat\tau_{\mathrm{med}}-\tau(P)|>u\}
 \leq\min\{1,10(K\rho_n/u)^3\}.                             \tag{61}
\]
For \(1\leq n<5\), thm:clipped-absolute-upper and Markov's inequality give
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|\widehat\tau_{\mathrm{med}}-\tau(P)|>C\rho_n/\alpha\}
 \leq\alpha.                                                  \tag{62}
\]
Choose \(K_\alpha\) larger than \(K(10/\alpha)^{1/3}\) and the finite-sample multiplier in (62).  By def:bias-aware-interval,
\[
 \inf_{\mathcal M_n}P\{\tau(P)\in\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}\}
 \geq1-\alpha,
 \quad\quad
 \operatorname{length}(\mathcal C_{n,\alpha})=2K_\alpha\rho_n.\tag{63}
\]
The center is measurable in \(O^{(n)}\) and uses only the inputs allowed by def:interval-rule-class, while the radius is deterministic given the certified inputs.  Hence \(\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}\in\mathcal I_n\), and (63) proves the asserted expected-length upper bound.

For the converse, first consider any two-point subexperiment in the proof of thm:four-channel-converse with targets separated by \(2d\) and sample total variation at most \(\gamma\).  Honesty implies that the interval contains the appropriate target with probability at least \(1-\alpha\) under each law.  Transfer the event that it contains the second target to the first law by total variation and intersect it with the event that it contains the first target.  This gives
\[
 P\{\operatorname{length}(C_n)\geq2d\}
 \geq1-2\alpha-\gamma.                                      \tag{64}
\]

For a fuzzy subexperiment, let the prior target under sign \(\theta\) lie within \(d/4\) of \(\mu_\theta\), except on prior probability \(\epsilon\), where \(|\mu_+-\mu_-|=2d\), and let the mixture sample laws have total variation at most \(\gamma\).  Honesty for every fixed law implies that, under either joint prior-and-sample law, the interval intersects the corresponding \(d/4\)-neighborhood with probability at least \(1-\alpha-\epsilon\).  Transfer one intersection event between the mixture laws and intersect the two events.  Because an interval meeting both neighborhoods has length at least \(3d/2\),
\[
 \overline P\{\operatorname{length}(C_n)\geq3d/2\}
 \geq1-2\alpha-\gamma-2\epsilon.                            \tag{65}
\]
The amplitude constants in the two-point channels and the cell count \(N\) in the fuzzy channels can be chosen, using only \(\alpha\) and the fixed class constants, so that \(\gamma+2\epsilon<(1-2\alpha)/2\).  Thus (64) or (65) makes the mixture expected length at least a positive \(\alpha\)-dependent constant times \(d\).  A mixture expectation is an average over the fixed-sign laws, all of which belong to \(\mathcal M_n\) by thm:four-channel-converse; hence at least one fixed law has that expected length.

Choose the lower-bound channel with the largest component in (55).  The maximum-to-sum comparison gives \(d\geq c\rho_n\) after a class-constant rescaling.  Therefore every uniformly honest \(C_n\in\mathcal I_n\) satisfies
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}E_P[\operatorname{length}(C_n)]
 \geq c_\alpha\rho_n.                                       \tag{66}
\]
Taking the infimum in def:bias-aware-interval and combining (63) with (66) proves \(\mathfrak L_{n,\alpha}\asymp\rho_n\).
